      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The 12% figure is a stark reality, but it's also a reflection of the fact that DEI initiatives are still in their infancy as a business discipline. It's not a scalable business model yet, but that doesn't mean it can't be. In fact, many of the most successful companies are now investing heavily in DEI research and development, trying to crack the code on how to measure and optimize their impact. Meanwhile, the cost of not investing in DEI is far higher - think about the lost talent, the missed opportunities, and the damage to your company's reputation. It's not a question of fiscal responsibility, but of business acumen. By investing in DEI, you're not just subsidizing a social movement, you're investing in your company's future.",
      "source": "target",
